From b187b265aada5075ed1c8551623993498979829a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kerotakis <cze@mailbox.org>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 06:22:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mol2000: the V2000 writer can say 'no hydrogens' and 'this
 isotope'

A coordinate-less molecule written to V2000 loses two statements a bracket
atom makes, and both change the structure a reader reconstructs:

* An explicit hydrogen count. `[Mg]` means elemental magnesium; the atom
  line's valence field stayed at 0 ('use the default valence'), so a reader
  that honours the periodic-table default -- the IUPAC InChI reference
  implementation among them -- reconstructs MgH2. `[C]` came back as
  methane and `[S]` as hydrogen sulfide. The valence field is the one
  channel V2000 has for this, with 15 as its spelling for zero, and it is
  now written for every bracket atom (organic-subset atoms keep 0: their
  hydrogen count *is* the default valence).

* The isotope. Nothing was ever emitted, so every isotopologue wrote out as
  its natural-abundance structure and heavy water became water. The atom
  line's mass-difference field only reaches -3..=+4 and is superseded
  wherever `M  ISO` appears, so the property line is what carries it.

Both writers (2D and the 3D-conformer one) are fixed together.
---
 crates/chematic-mol/src/mol2000.rs | 128 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 123 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/crates/chematic-mol/src/mol2000.rs b/crates/chematic-mol/src/mol2000.rs
index 7ffeb0c..4f7f652 100644
--- a/crates/chematic-mol/src/mol2000.rs
+++ b/crates/chematic-mol/src/mol2000.rs
